  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The model's interpretation and predictions rely on Uber Movement data as the ground truth, which may contain inherent sampling biases toward specific times or driver demographics.",
      "what": "Researchers developed a two-stage framework combining a GraphSAGE neural network with Geographical and Temporal Weighted Regression (GTWR) to predict and interpret street-level traffic speeds across Berlin, Germany. Using Uber Movement speed data as the target variable alongside OpenStreetMap road attributes, population density, and street-view-derived activity indicators, the model predicted speeds for the 72% of the road network lacking empirical data with a mean absolute error of 5.73 km/h. The GTWR post-hoc analysis identified four spatial clusters of feature importance, showing that road type and speed limits increased predicted speeds while population density, pedestrian counts, and traffic signals decreased them.",
      "why": "The framework provides a method to interpret the black-box predictions of graph neural networks in urban transport, mapping how the model shifts its reliance on different spatial features across different neighborhoods. This spatial clustering of model behavior offers a structured pathway for transferring traffic prediction models to other cities where empirical speed data is missing."
    }
  },
